Torpor-Strut crawled out of the salt-encrusted grain terminals and sub-Arctic dry docks of Port Churchill on Hudson Bay during the bitter winter of 1956. Displaced Caribbean and Harlem-bound merchant mariners brought back early sound-system boasting and street-corner rhyming routines, colliding head-on with local Cree dockers and northern boilermakers trapped inside unheated reinforced-concrete naval silos. Faced with sub-zero isolation, they discarded rock-and-roll upbeat bounce, tuning their pacing to the slow metabolic shutdowns of denned black bears and wintering ringed seals whose slowed, irregular thuds could be heard through timber hulls. The scene’s dress code embraced brutal utility: greased wool peacoats stenciled with hand-carved harbor symbols, shearling mittens with sliced fingertips for holding copper megaphones, and heavy steel-capped caulk boots slammed against loading-bay decking. These "Choke-Jams" were clandestine tests of breath and stamina, held around coal braziers until the city icebreakers arrived in spring.

